For unit vectors $q$ and $k$, the triangle inequality gives $\|q-k\|\leq\|q\|+\|k\|=2$. Hence $1+\|q-k\|^2\leq5$, and reciprocation gives $K(q,k)\geq1/5$. Therefore, if $	au\leq1/5$, every key satisfies $K(q,k)\geq	au$.

</details>

## See why the square-root claim fails

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

The exact active count is $N$, whereas the benchmark is $\lfloor\sqrt Nfloor$. Their ratio grows like $\sqrt N$.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the cardinality argument</summary>

Because every key is active, the active set equals the complete key set and has cardinality $N$. For $N>1$, $\lfloor\sqrt Nfloor<N$. More generally, $N>C\sqrt N$ once $N>C^2$, so no fixed constant $C$ yields a uniform $C\sqrt N$ bound in this threshold regime.

</details>

## Repair the mechanism with a bandwidth

A scale parameter changes the kernel to $K_ eta(q,k)=1/(1+ eta\|q-k\|^2)$. Its floor is $1/(1+4 eta)$, and its active region is a spherical cap.

